  "id": "F1",
  "title": "The growth diagnostic's clock can fail cleanup and is read on every child exit",
  "file": "src/util/proc.ts",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "On Linux, if the process cannot read its own /proc/self/auxv or /proc/uptime, or the kernel does not report the clock-tick rate, every command's cleanup fails as soon as the command exits, even when nothing is left to stop. Later commands in that invocation are then refused. The input that fixes this clock is used only to word an error message, and normal Linux systems always provide it.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "Throwaway bun test under $TMPDIR/f1check: run sh([\"true\"], {cwd}) inside processBirthInspection.run({read: processBirth, now: () => { throw new Error(\"clock unavailable\") }}), then again with a working now()",
    "output": "rejected: Invocation termination could not be confirmed: clock unavailable\nok"
   },
   "evidence": "At head, src/util/proc.ts:267 `const bornAt = (processBirthInspection.getStore()?.now ?? birthClock)();` runs before the try and before any discovery, on every child exit. birthClock (241-252) on Linux calls readFileSync(\"/proc/self/auxv\") and readFileSync(\"/proc/uptime\") and throws \"Process birth clock could not be confirmed\" when AT_CLKTCK is missing. The throw reaches terminate() (proc.ts ~506-517), which wraps it as ProcessTerminationError and sets scope.terminationError. Base had no clock read. The real Linux path can't run on this darwin host, so I injected the failure through the test hook. The clock's only use is the growth diagnostic. On real Linux these files are always readable when /proc (which discovery needs anyway) is mounted, so the trigger is unusual. The cost of two small synchronous procfs reads per exit is negligible."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "On a Linux installation where diagnostic clock metadata is unavailable, a command finishes successfully but the factory reports an unconfirmed shutdown. It blocks subsequent commands in that run and requires intervention despite there being no surviving processes.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun \"${TMPDIR}/adjudicate-537.BvpMlh/probe.ts\"",
    "output": "HEAD was bundled with its Linux backend selected and fixture filesystem reads; ancestry and empty discovery were injected. auxv-unreadable: scans=0, latched=true, both commands rejected with ProcessTerminationError: Invocation termination could not be confirmed: EACCES: /proc/self/auxv. uptime-unreadable: scans=0, latched=true, both commands rejected with the corresponding /proc/uptime error. missing-AT_CLKTCK: scans=0, latched=true, both commands rejected with Process birth clock could not be confirmed. Readable control: scans=4, latched=false, outcomes=[\"exit=0\",\"exit=0\"], with auxv and uptime each read twice."
   },
   "evidence": "HEAD unconditionally executes `const bornAt = (processBirthInspection.getStore()?.now ?? birthClock)();` before its discovery loop and outside its cleanup try/catch. birthClock reads `/proc/self/auxv` and `/proc/uptime`, and throws when AT_CLKTCK is missing. runProcess converts that exception into ProcessTerminationError and assigns `scope.terminationError`; assertProcessesStopped subsequently throws the latched error. Base had no birthClock dependency and could complete empty discovery without those files. The reproduction uses filesystem fixtures on macOS, rather than demonstrating an actual Linux permission restriction."
  }
 },
 {
  "id": "F2",
  "title": "Growth comparison ignores the clocks' precision",
  "file": "src/util/proc.ts",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "When cleanup cannot finish, a process born within the clock\u2019s rounding interval can make the error incorrectly report continued forking. The operator receives a misleading explanation, although process termination itself is unaffected by this comparison.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun \"${TMPDIR}/adjudicate-537.BvpMlh/probe.ts\"",
    "output": "Using injected birth identities, an accelerated deadline, and suppressed signals exclusively to an owned sleep: Darwin clock=1792169203023000, birth=\"1792169203:23704\" produced \"Marked processes still alive: 1: <owned-pid> (sleep); set kept growing\". Linux clock=1002.9999999999999, birth=\"1003\" produced the same growth diagnostic. Separately, 10.03*100 evaluated to 1002.9999999999999."
   },
   "evidence": "HEAD uses `Date.now() * 1000` for Darwin, while native births retain microseconds. Consequently, a birth 704 microseconds into the sampled millisecond passes `growing ||= birthTime(birth) > bornAt` even though it can precede the sample. Linux computes the clock with floating-point multiplication, so the reproduced same-tick birth also passes that comparison. Only the final diagnostic selects wording using growing; signalling and deadline failure do not depend on it. Base had neither this comparison nor the growth diagnostic."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "When cleanup gives up on a stable set of processes and one of them started in the same millisecond (macOS) or clock tick (Linux) just before cleanup began, the error says the set \"kept growing\" instead of \"remained stable\". Whoever reads it may blame a forking tree rather than a slow machine. Only the wording of the failure message is affected.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun -e 'console.log(10.03*100, 1003 > 10.03*100); const b=\"1792169203:23704\"; console.log(b.split(\":\").reduce((t,f)=>t*1e6+Number(f),0) > 1792169203023*1000)'",
    "output": "1002.9999999999999 true\ntrue"
   },
   "evidence": "At head, src/util/proc.ts:243 `if (inspectionPlatform === \"darwin\") return Date.now() * 1000;` gives a millisecond-truncated clock, while darwinProcessBirth returns `sec:usec` and birthTime converts it to microseconds. Line 248-250 computes Number(uptime) * hz in floating point, so 10.03 * 100 = 1002.9999999999999. Line 278 `growing ||= birthTime(birth) > bornAt;` uses a strict compare with no allowance for either clock's precision. The flag appears only in the deadline error's \"kept growing\"/\"remained stable\" suffix (lines ~312-315)."
  }
 }
